{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# DC rate-limit headers are not honoured; a 429 is a hard failure\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nJira Data Center has built-in rate limiting (Administration → System → Rate limiting), introduced in\n**8.6**, **Data Center only**, and **OFF BY DEFAULT** — an administrator enables it. It is a per-user\nTOKEN BUCKET with a fill rate, not a fixed window.\n\nrebar's DC transport does not handle it at all. `_with_connection_retry` retries only\nconnection-level faults and its docstring states explicitly that `jira.exceptions.JIRAError` — \"any\nHTTP 4xx/5xx response\" — is NOT in the retry set. A 429 therefore propagates as a hard failure on\nthe first occurrence, aborting whatever read or mutation was in flight, on an instance that was\nmerely asking rebar to slow down.\n\n**Scope decision by the operator:** this IS in scope for epic e369. The counter-argument that it is\nan absent FEATURE rather than a defect (off by default, DC-only, admin-enabled) was weighed and\nrejected: an integration that falls over on a rate-limited instance is broken FOR THAT CUSTOMER, and\n\"the admin has to enable it\" is a thin defence when Data Center exists precisely for large\nself-hosted deployments — the ones most likely to turn throttling on.\n\n## Approach — THIS TICKET IS NOW A PARENT; THE WORK IS IN TWO CHILDREN\n\n**This plan carried TWO independent changes and BLOCKED ten times because of it.** The full\nround-by-round record is in this ticket's comments. The structural finding: rounds 6-7 were about\nthe MECHANICS of relocating code, while rounds 1, 4, 8, 9 and 10 were about the 429 POLICY, and each\nround a fix to one re-opened surface in the other. Handoff lesson (epic e369): when a plan keeps\nreturning findings, suspect STRUCTURE, not wording — J9 blocked eight times and splitting it into\nJ10 produced a first-try PASS.\n\nSplit accordingly, and the implementation detail now lives in the children rather than here:\n\n* **[rebar:f2f3-9cb1-335b-4e31] — S1: extract the retry/error cluster out of `transport.py`.** A\n  pure, behaviour-preserving relocation, FORCED by the locked module-size cap: `transport.py` is\n  789 lines against the 800 cap, so eleven lines of headroom mean the policy cannot be added in\n  place. Verifiable by reading the diff and two `wc -l`. **PASSED plan review first try**, which is\n  the evidence the split was the right move.\n* **[rebar:6758-26b8-c9ea-4c5d] — S2: add the 429 policy in the extracted module.** Reuses\n  `_errors.parse_retry_after` / `MAX_BACKOFF_S`, applies jitter-then-clamp, and scopes the retry to\n  IDEMPOTENT READS behind a per-call opt-in defaulting to OFF.\n\n**S2 `depends_on` S1** — recorded as a link, not merely stated, so `next-batch` withholds S2 until\nS1 closes.\n\n**The constraint that shapes S2, restated here because it is the one that must not be lost in the\nsplit:** `_with_connection_retry` is the single choke point for ALL 15 transport call sites\nINCLUDING `create_issue`. Retrying 429s there would REINTRODUCE the duplicate-issue class\n[rebar:21fc-51d7-90ca-4a03] just fixed — a 429 can arrive after the server began the write, and\nnothing in the response distinguishes that from rejection at the gate.\n\n## Scope\n\nNo code changes land under THIS ticket. Its scope is the decomposition and the two children above.\n\n## Testing\n\nThe children carry the behavioural tests. This ticket's own verification is that the decomposition\nis complete and correctly ordered — checkable from the ticket graph, not from a test run.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] both children exist, are children of this ticket, and between them cover the whole original\n      scope: the relocation (S1) and the 429 policy (S2), with nothing from the original plan\n      orphaned\n- [x] S2 depends_on S1 as a recorded link, verified by `next-batch` withholding S2 while S1 is open\n- [x] the duplicate-risk constraint (429 retry must NOT reach mutations, because\n      `_with_connection_retry` fronts `create_issue`) appears in S2's plan, so the split cannot lose\n      the finding that took four review rounds to establish\n- [x] the ten-round review record stays on this ticket as comments, so the reason for the split is\n      recoverable by the next reader rather than being rediscovered\n- [x] both children are CLOSED before this ticket closes\n\n## Criteria verified — see RECLOSE-B586-A\n\nAC1 / AC5: S1 `f2f3-9cb1-335b-4e31` and S2 `6758-26b8-c9ea-4c5d` are now children of this ticket and\nboth are CLOSED and `certified`/`valid`. They were ORPHANS (`parent_id = ''`) until this pass — the\nstructure these criteria assert did not exist in the store, and re-parenting restored what the split\nintended. `child_closure_findings(b586)` reports 0 blocking, 0 uncertified.\n\nAC2: `6758 depends_on f2f3` is a recorded link in S2's deps.\n\nAC3: the four-round finding survives in S2's plan as \"SCOPE THE RETRY TO IDEMPOTENT READS. This is the\nload-bearing constraint\", naming `_with_connection_retry` as the choke point for all 15 call sites\nincluding `create_issue`, plus a per-member AC that every mutation site still fails on the first 429.\n\nAC4: the ten-round review record remains here as comments."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785876865048166001,"uuid":"94e3099b-d6b2-4b2f-9a18-c2ea8a0b0810"}